<interrogationes unitas="capsula">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I embed asset files in a binary and read them at runtime?
    <responsum symbolum="capsula_aperire capsula_legere CapsulaEmbed" via="tools/capsula_generare.c probationes/test_capsula.toml tools/capsula_regenerare.sh">List
      the files in a TOML file as top-level keys X_files = [...] (paths
      or * glob patterns, relative to the TOML's directory) and
      X_compress = true|false (default false). Not [X] tables with
      files/compress, whatever the generator's top comment and usage
      text say: those generate nothing. bin/capsula_generare writes
      capsula_X.c/.h next to the TOML, defining constans CapsulaEmbed
      capsula_X; tools/capsula_regenerare.sh runs it. At runtime
      capsula_aperire(&amp;capsula_X, piscina) then capsula_legere(cap,
      "path/relative/to/the/toml", piscina), checking .status ==
      CAPSULA_OK.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what do I get back from capsula_legere, and who owns the bytes?
    <responsum symbolum="capsula_legere capsula_legere_chorda CapsulaFructus">A
      CapsulaFructus: status plus datum, a chorda (NOT NUL-terminated,
      use chorda_ut_cstr if you need a C string). Every call allocates a
      fresh copy (or a fresh decompression) in the piscina you pass,
      even for uncompressed entries, because the embedded blob is const.
      Reading the same file in a loop therefore grows that piscina;
      read once and keep the chorda.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I serve assets from a directory during development without rebuilding?
    <responsum symbolum="capsula_aperire_e_disco capsula_legere capsula_habet" via="lib/atrium.c">capsula_aperire_e_disco(radix,
      piscina) returns a Capsula with the same handle API, reading the
      directory instead of the blob (atrium uses it for -radix).
      capsula_legere, _chorda and capsula_habet go to disk on every
      call, so an edited file is seen at once. It returns NIHIL if radix
      is not a directory. Paths that normalize outside radix ("../x")
      are refused as CAPSULA_NON_INVENTUM. Nothing is compressed. An
      existing empty file is CAPSULA_OK with mensura 0. Note that in
      this mode capsula_habet builds the full path in the piscina given
      to capsula_aperire_e_disco on every call (it takes no piscina of
      its own), so calling it per request slowly grows that
      piscina. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): if a file appears
      under radix while capsula_aperire_e_disco is opening it (between
      its counting walk and its filling walk), the fill writes one
      CapsulaIndexum past the end of the index allocation, a heap
      overflow in the piscina.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I list files that were added after capsula_aperire_e_disco opened the directory?
    <responsum symbolum="capsula_aperire_e_disco capsula_numerus capsula_indexum capsula_iter capsula_invenire capsula_legere">No.
      capsula_numerus, capsula_indexum, capsula_iter and capsula_invenire
      use the index taken when the directory was opened. A file added
      later can be read with capsula_legere but is not listed until you
      open it again. The index also skips hidden entries (dot-files),
      though capsula_legere still reads them by path.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I find out why capsula_aperire failed?
    <responsum symbolum="capsula_aperire capsula_status_nuntium CapsulaStatus">No:
      capsula_aperire returns only NIHIL, whether embed or piscina is
      NIHIL, the blob is under XII bytes, or the magic or version is
      wrong. CAPSULA_FRACTA_MAGICA and CAPSULA_FRACTA_VERSIO are never
      produced anywhere, and CAPSULA_FRACTA_DATUM only by the separate
      capsula_caudae library; in capsula.c only capsula_legere and
      capsula_legere_chorda return a status (CAPSULA_NON_INVENTUM,
      CAPSULA_FRACTA_INFLATIO, CAPSULA_FRACTA_ALLOCATIO).
      capsula_status_nuntium turns a status into text. The TOC is not
      bounds-checked against the blob, so only open blobs that
      capsula_generare produced.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I walk every file in a capsula?
    <responsum symbolum="capsula_numerus capsula_indexum CapsulaIndexum capsula_legere_chorda" via="lib/speculum.c">Loop
      i from 0 to capsula_numerus(cap) - 1, take capsula_indexum(cap, i)
      and read it with capsula_legere_chorda(cap, entry-&gt;via,
      piscina), as lib/speculum.c does. capsula_iter and
      capsula_iter_proximus also exist, but after proximus the current
      entry is capsula_indexum(cap, iter.positus - 1): positus is
      1-based once advanced.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I tell whether an entry is stored compressed, and what is mensura_compressa for an uncompressed file?
    <responsum symbolum="CapsulaIndexum capsula_aperire capsula_aperire_e_disco" via="tools/capsula_generare.c">Read
      the compressa flag, not mensura_compressa. The header says
      mensura_compressa is 0 when uncompressed, but for an embedded
      capsula the generator writes it EQUAL to mensura_cruda and
      capsula_aperire sets compressa = (mensura_compressa !=
      mensura_cruda); only disk mode stores 0. mensura_cruda is the
      uncompressed size (in disk mode, the size when the index was
      taken; capsula_legere returns the file as it is now).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does disk mode exist instead of serving assets over a local HTTP server?
    <responsum symbolum="capsula_aperire_e_disco">The page keeps the same
      scheme, URL and origin (capsula://radix/...) and cannot tell
      which mode it runs in, so development and release differ only in
      which files exist. An HTTP server would also change port, origin,
      transport and cwd. And nothing listens: no port that any process
      on the machine could read (header comment on
      capsula_aperire_e_disco).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
